## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates mastery of fixed-popcount permutations. Tests carry propagation and ability to explain a one-shot solution without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Snoob algorithm for next with same popcount
- Carry ripple and bitwise masks
- Edge-case handling for zero or maximal permutations

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t nextBitPermutation64(uint64_t x) {
    // Assumes x != 0 and not the maximal permutation for its popcount
    uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
    uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
    uint64_t ones = x ^ ripple;
    ones = (ones >> 2) / smallest;
    return ripple | ones;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to arbitrary bit-widths?
- Compare this approach to a loop-based generator in terms of performance and readability.

## Why This Is Asked

Parity computation over 128 bits tests the ability to design constant-time reductions using only bitwise operations and shifts, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise XOR folding across 128 bits
- Progressive reduction by shifting (64,32,16,8,4,2,1)
- Final extraction with a single bit AND

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128_t;
unsigned parity128(uint128_t x) {
  uint64_t v = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  v ^= v >> 32;
  v ^= v >> 16;
  v ^= v >> 8;
  v ^= v >> 4;
  v ^= v >> 2;
  v ^= v >> 1;
  return v & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256 bits while preserving the same approach?
- Can you prove the operation is constant time regardless of input bit distribution?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design branchless bit tricks for palindrome detection on 64-bit values, a common bitboard problem.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR-based bit reversal
- Branchless boolean results
- Fixed-width masking patterns

## Code Example

```javascript
function reverse64(n) {
  n = ((n >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  n = ((n >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  n = ((n >> 4n) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n);
  n = ((n >> 8n) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n);
  n = ((n >> 16n) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) | ((n & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n);
  n = (n >> 32n) | (n << 32n);
  return n;
}
function isPalindrome64(n) {
  return reverse64(n) === n;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

- Tests ability to implement a canonical bit-counting trick without loops, emphasizing constant-time operations. 
- Demonstrates careful use of masks and shifts to aggregate counts across bit groups.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (parallel bit counting)
- Bit masks 0x55555555, 0x33333333, 0x0F0F0F0F
- Constant-time arithmetic with shifts

## Code Example

```javascript
function popcount32(x) {
  x = x - ((x >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >>> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  return (x * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to 64-bit inputs?
- Compare this approach to using hardware popcount instructions in terms of portability and performance.

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a branchless, constant-time population-count across 128 bits using only shifts and masks, a common low-level optimization in crypto/signature code.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register)
- 64-bit popcount, extended to 128-bit
- Branchless arithmetic for performance

## Code Example

```c
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t w) {
  w = w - ((w >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  w = (w & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((w >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  w = (w + (w >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (unsigned int)((w * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56);
}
unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  unsigned int hi = popcount64(x.hi);
  unsigned int lo = popcount64(x.lo);
  return hi + lo;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for variable-width counts (e.g., 256-bit)?
- What are the trade-offs compared to using hardware popcnt when available?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128 bit manipulations and zero edge cases without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Branchless bit tricks
- 128 bit layout as hi and lo
- De Bruijn based ctz for 64 and 32 bit values
- Handling zero input gracefully

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline int ctz64(uint64_t x) {
  if (x == 0) return 64;
  static const int tab[64] = { /* 64 entries */ };
  uint64_t y = x & -x;
  return tab[(y * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ULL) >> 58];
}

int lsbIndex128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  int a = ctz64(lo);
  int b = 64 + ctz64(hi);
  int m = - (int)(lo != 0);
  int res = (a & m) | (b & ~m);
  return (lo | hi) == 0 ? -1 : res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if 128 bit intrinsics are unavailable
- Extend to msbIndex128 with branchless patterns

## Why This Is Asked
Tests bitboard reasoning, edge handling, and efficient parallelism using only bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts
- Bitboard edge wrapping and file masks
- Parallel neighbor computation via multiple shifts
- Safe, branch-free bitwise design

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t neighborMask64(uint64_t board) {
    const uint64_t notA = 0xfefefefefefefefeULL;
    const uint64_t notH = 0x7f7f7f7f7f7f7f7fULL;
    uint64_t east  = (board & notH) << 1;
    uint64_t west  = (board & notA) >> 1;
    uint64_t south = board << 8;
    uint64_t north = board >> 8;
    uint64_t ne = (board & notH) >> 7;
    uint64_t nw = (board & notA) >> 9;
    uint64_t se = (board & notH) << 9;
    uint64_t sw = (board & notA) << 7;
    return east | west | south | north | ne | nw | se | sw;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a 32-bit board layout?
- How would you unit-test edge cases (corners and edges) efficiently?

