## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to unpack bit-packed fields without branches—a common low-level task in networking, codecs, and embedded systems.

## Key Concepts

- **Bitwise masks and shifts** extract subfields from packed integers efficiently
- **No branches** ensures constant-time execution, critical for performance-sensitive code
- **Endianness** is irrelevant when masking at exact bit positions

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
    uint8_t  version;
    uint8_t  type;
    uint16_t length;
    uint8_t  flags;
} Header;

Header extractHeaderFields(uint32_t w) {
    Header h;
    h.version = (w >> 28) & 0xF;  // Bits 31-28
    h.type    = (w >> 24) & 0xF;  // Bits 27-24
    h.length  = (w >> 8)  & 0xFFFF; // Bits 23-8
    h.flags   = w & 0xFF;          // Bits 7-0
    return h;
}
```

Each shift moves the target field to the least significant position, then the mask isolates the relevant bits. This approach is branchless, deterministic, and executes in constant time.

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes fluency with SWAR bit-twiddling and 128-bit composition, ensuring branchless correctness.

## Key Concepts
- 64-bit SWAR reversal
- 128-bit result from {hi: rev64(lo), lo: rev64(hi)}
- Mask/shift sequences to flip bit blocks without branches

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t rev64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) | (x << 32);
  return x;
}

uint128 reverseBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = rev64(x.lo);
  r.lo = rev64(x.hi);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test edge alignment and performance implications?
- Could this approach be generalized to arbitrary widths with minimal changes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply bit-hacking techniques across a 128-bit value by splitting into two 64-bit halves and summing, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts

- 64-bit popcount via parallel bit counting masks
- Treat 128-bit as two 64-bit blocks
- No loops or branches; purely bitwise ops

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return x & 0x7f;
}
unsigned int countBits128(uint128 x){
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you leverage __builtin_popcountll if available?
- How would you generalize to 256-bit types?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to scale classic SWAR nibble-swaps to 128-bit values, ensuring implementations stay constant-time and border-safe.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit lane-wise bit-twiddling
- SWAR-style mask-and-shift patterns
- Safe handling of hi/lo halves

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 nibbleSwap128(uint128 x) {
  const uint64_t M = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = ((x.lo & M) << 4) | ((x.lo >> 4) & M);
  r.hi = ((x.hi & M) << 4) | ((x.hi >> 4) & M);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to 256-bit types? 
- How do you test with all-zero and all-0xFF inputs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests endianness understanding and safe 128-bit lane manipulation with pure bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts
- Byte reversal with masks
- 128-bit struct arithmetic
- Endianness swapping

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline uint64_t reverseBytes64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  x = (x << 32) | (x >> 32);
  return x;
}

uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = reverseBytes64(x.lo);
  r.lo = reverseBytes64(x.hi);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a compile-time constant shift? 
- How to verify with unit tests across edge cases (all zeros, all ones, cross-boundary values)?

## Why This Is Asked
Fresh bit-hack that demonstrates mastery of fixed-step SWAR technique and portability across languages.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR fixed-masks and staged swaps
- Bitwise rotation-free reversal
- Unsigned 32-bit wrap handling in JavaScript/TypeScript

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverseBits32(n) {
  n = ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555) | ((n & 0x55555555) << 1);
  n = ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333) | ((n & 0x33333333) << 2);
  n = ((n >>> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);
  n = ((n >>> 8) & 0x00FF00FF) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF) << 8);
  n = (n >>> 16) | (n << 16);
  return n >>> 0;
}
```

